states.description:
The “STATES” cycle consists of four mask collections exploring the human condition through the elements: fire, earth, water, and air. The mask is one of the oldest artistic and ritual objects. It does not conceal — it reveals an inner state.

Each element appears not as a force of nature, but as a psychological condition and a stage of inner transformation:
— fire — confrontation with the shadow;
— earth — a return to the body and materiality;
— water — immersion into the subconscious and the loss of stable identity;
— air — the dissolution of form and transition into pure state.

“STATES” presents the human being not as a fixed identity, but as a shifting system of states. Each element destroys the previous form, transforming identity in the process.

The masks are created from papier-mâché combined with natural materials and textiles. Recycled paper becomes a metaphor for processed experience, memory, and repressed states. The visible fibers and irregular textures of the material remain as scars of lived experience.

These objects are not decorative or festival masks. They are created for contemplation, inner dialogue, and encounters with archetypal human states.

The collection is limited to seven masks. Seven is a number associated with transition and inner initiation. Each mask represents a distinct archetype and a distinct state of transformation.
